 leaf, was rejected as peripheral learner vocabulary for this slang sense. The closure review recommends that same rejected tag, which cannot be resubmitted under the current nonduplication rule. No other supplied leaf precisely fits feet.\".\npass only if a learner''s dictionary such as the Oxford Advanced Learner''s, Longman, or Cambridge would genuinely have nothing for this slot, so leaving it empty is complete. For example:\n- months, days, and numbers have no common collocations or synonyms beyond the names of their set\n- a verb with fewer than three common phrasal verbs or verb idioms, when the reason names the ones that exist (\"describe\" has none)\n- a word with no common derivatives (\"notebook\", \"salad\"), or a function word (\"and\", \"with\")\n- a sense whose recorded relations are the only precise ones: no further synonym, antonym, broader term, or whole would help a learner\n- an uncountable noun (\"advice\") has no plural\n- a title used before a name (\"Mr\", \"Mrs\", \"Ms\", \"Dr\") has no grammar to record: a learner''s dictionary prints it as used before a name, never [C] or [U], so no countability fits it\n- a pronoun that governs no agreement has no grammar to record: an object, reflexive, or reciprocal pronoun (\"me\", \"yourself\", \"each other\") is never the subject a verb agrees with, and a relative or interrogative pronoun (\"who\", \"which\", \"what\") takes the verb its antecedent or answer takes, so no fixed agreement fits it\n- an adverb used only straight after a number, a clock time, or a measure (\"10 a.m.\", \"children aged five and below\") has no clause position to record: front, mid, and end describe where an adverb goes in a clause, not after a number\n- a non-gradable adjective (\"main\", \"dead\") has no comparative\n- a function word, a general word (\"thing\", \"get\"), or a general-purpose sense usable in many scenes belongs to no topic, even where it can appear in one\nfail when the slot plainly applies to this word, so a learner would expect it filled, or the reason is wrong. For example:\n- a common verb lacks inflections, or a noun its plural, that a learner can look up in any dictionary\n- a verb with common phrasal verbs the reason leaves out (\"throw\" has throw away, throw out, and throw up), or a word with common derivatives (\"happy\" has happiness)\n- a word with ordinary synonyms, collocations, or a topic only someone has not found yet\n- a reason that is wrong, vague, or only says the data is hard to find\nA fail reason names the specific value a learner''s dictionary gives for the slot, never only that one exists.\nReview already rejected these attempts to fill this slot, most recent first:\n- topic {\"topic_id\":\"body-parts\"} (topic \"Parts of the body\" (in the category \"Body and health\")): rejected because \"This slang term for feet is too peripheral to be core vocabulary for learners studying parts of the body.\"\nEach was judged not to fit. Never fail for a value one of them tried, or for an association as loose as theirs: when the attempts were rejected as not fitting, pass unless you can name a specific value that plainly fills the slot and differs from every rejected one.\nuncertain: only when you cannot judge the word at all.\nOther senses of this entry, for reference:\n- (noun) \"A domesticated animal with four legs, a tail, and a barking voice, often kept as a pet or trained for work.\"\n- (noun) \"A male dog or a male of a related animal such as a fox.\"\n- (noun) \"A person, especially a man, regarded as unpleasant or contemptible.\"\n- (noun) \"Something of very poor quality or something that is a commercial failure.\"\n- (verb) \"To follow someone closely and persistently.\"\n- (verb) \"To cause persistent trouble or difficulty for someone or something.\"\n- (noun) \"A woman considered physically unattractive.\"\n- (noun) \"A man described in a familiar or affectionate way as having a particular quality.\"\n- (noun) \"A hot dog.\"\n- (noun) \"Greyhound racing, especially as an activity involving betting.\""}},"context":{"subject":{"lemma":"dog","pos":"noun","gloss":"A person''s feet.","siblings":[{"pos":"noun","gloss":"A domesticated animal with four legs, a tail, and a barking voice, often kept as a pet or trained for work."},{"pos":"noun","gloss":"A male dog or a male of a related animal such as a fox."},{"pos":"noun","gloss":"A person, especially a man, regarded as unpleasant or contemptible."},{"pos":"noun","gloss":"Something of very poor quality or something that is a commercial failure."},{"pos":"verb","gloss":"To follow someone closely and persistently."},{"pos":"verb","gloss":"To cause persistent trouble or difficulty for someone or something."},{"pos":"noun","gloss":"A woman considered physically unattractive."},{"pos":"noun","gloss":"A man described in a familiar or affectionate way as having a particular quality."},{"pos":"noun","gloss":"A hot dog."},{"pos":"noun","gloss":"Greyhound racing, especially as an activity involving betting."}]},"target":null,"topic":null,"attempts":[{"kind":"topic","payload":{"topic_id":"body-parts"},"word":null,"other":null,"topic":{"category":"Body and health","topic":"Parts of the body"},"reason":"This slang term for feet is too peripheral to be core vocabulary for learners studying parts of the body."}]},"reviewed_at":"2026-11-10T18:23:42.867Z"}', '2026-11-10T18:23:30.332Z', '2026-11-10T18:23:42.867Z', NULL);
