al senses count, and so do glosses written as usage notes (\"Used before … to …\"). Minor paraphrase is fine.\nfail: the word has no such meaning in this part of speech, the gloss describes a different word or a proper name, or it is a one-off or invented use, or the sense is not used in this register.\nA sense used only in one variety of English states its region: fail such a sense without one (\"lorry\", \"queue\" as a verb, \"flat\" meaning an apartment, and \"mobile\" meaning a mobile phone are british; \"truck\" meaning a lorry, \"sidewalk\", and \"cell\" meaning a phone are american; \"arvo\" is australian; \"stay\" meaning to live somewhere permanently is scottish), and fail a region that does not fit. A sense used in British and American English alike states none.\nRegister labels follow learner''s dictionaries, where dated is the label they write as old-fashioned: fail a sense used only in a formal, literary, dated (old-fashioned), or ironic register that states no register or another one (\"fine\" meaning not good at all, as in \"a fine friend you are\", is ironic), and fail a sense they label old-fashioned that is not labelled dated.\nOther senses of this entry, for reference:\n- (adjective) \"Having a low temperature or a temperature lower than is comfortable.\"\n- (adjective) \"Feeling a lack of warmth in the body.\"\n- (adjective) \"Served without being heated or after being allowed to cool.\"\n- (adjective) \"Showing little affection, sympathy, or friendliness.\"\n- (adjective) \"Based on facts and reasoning without the influence of emotion.\"\n- (adjective) \"No longer fresh enough to provide useful clues about someone or something being sought.\"\n- (adjective) \"Far from finding a hidden object or guessing the correct answer in a game.\"\n- (adjective) \"Giving an impression of coolness through blue, green, or grey tones.\"\n- (adjective) \"Unconscious.\"\n- (noun) \"A common viral infection of the nose and throat that causes sneezing, a runny nose, and sometimes a cough.\"\n- (noun) \"A low temperature, especially in the weather or surrounding air.\"\n- (adverb) \"Without preparation, practice, or prior knowledge.\"\n- (adverb) \"Suddenly and completely.\"\n- (adjective) \"Done without preparation or prior knowledge.\"\n- (adjective) \"Showing no emotion or pity when harming others.\"\n- (adjective) \"Unpleasant or depressing because it offers no comfort or hope.\""}}}]},"context":{"subject":{"lemma":"cold","pos":"adverb","gloss":"Completely and accurately from memory.","siblings":[{"pos":"adjective","gloss":"Having a low temperature or a temperature lower than is comfortable."},{"pos":"adjective","gloss":"Feeling a lack of warmth in the body."},{"pos":"adjective","gloss":"Served without being heated or after being allowed to cool."},{"pos":"adjective","gloss":"Showing little affection, sympathy, or friendliness."},{"pos":"adjective","gloss":"Based on facts and reasoning without the influence of emotion."},{"pos":"adjective","gloss":"No longer fresh enough to provide useful clues about someone or something being sought."},{"pos":"adjective","gloss":"Far from finding a hidden object or guessing the correct answer in a game."},{"pos":"adjective","gloss":"Giving an impression of coolness through blue, green, or grey tones."},{"pos":"adjective","gloss":"Unconscious."},{"pos":"noun","gloss":"A common viral infection of the nose and throat that causes sneezing, a runny nose, and sometimes a cough."},{"pos":"noun","gloss":"A low temperature, especially in the weather or surrounding air."},{"pos":"adverb","gloss":"Without preparation, practice, or prior knowledge."},{"pos":"adverb","gloss":"Suddenly and completely."},{"pos":"adjective","gloss":"Done without preparation or prior knowledge."},{"pos":"adjective","gloss":"Showing no emotion or pity when harming others."},{"pos":"adjective","gloss":"Unpleasant or depressing because it offers no comfort or hope."}]},"target":null,"topic":null,"attempts":[]},"reviewed_at":