uplicates, or one meaning split too finely into several.\n- Ordered: common runs from the most to the least common use of the word.\n- Rare: every sense in extra is genuinely rare, archaic, technical, or specialist, not one a learner meets in general use.\nA rare, archaic, Old English, technical, or specialist sense missing from both lists is never a failure.\nfail when any of them does not hold. The reason then names everything to change, even if that takes more than one sentence: each missing common sense with its part of speech and a short meaning, each pair of duplicates by number, each sense out of order, and each sense in extra that is common.\nuncertain: you cannot tell."}},"context":{"subject":{"lemma":"cat","senses":[{"id":"af217c59-c334-4cec-b4a4-2bcf923341d4","pos":"noun","gloss":"A small domesticated animal with soft fur, whiskers, and retractable claws, often kept as a pet."},{"id":"eef6d786-77bb-47fa-8c96-8a2c82c112b3","pos":"noun","gloss":"A wild animal of the same family as the domestic cat, including lions, tigers, and leopards."}],"inflectionOf":[],"cefr":[{"level":"A1","pos":"noun","source":"CEFR-J Wordlist"}]},"target":null,"topic":null},"reviewed_at":"2026-11-10T00:37:52.700Z"}', '2026-11-10T00:37:31.751Z', '2026-11-10T00:37:52.700Z', NULL);
