fore … to …\"). Minor paraphrase is fine.\nfail: the word has no such meaning in this part of speech, the gloss describes a different word or a proper name, or it is a one-off or invented use, or the sense is not used in this register.\nA sense used only in one variety of English states its region: fail such a sense without one (\"lorry\", \"queue\" as a verb, \"flat\" meaning an apartment, and \"mobile\" meaning a mobile phone are british; \"truck\" meaning a lorry, \"sidewalk\", and \"cell\" meaning a phone are american; \"arvo\" is australian; \"stay\" meaning to live somewhere permanently is scottish), and fail a region that does not fit. A sense used in British and American English alike states none.\nRegister labels follow learner''s dictionaries, where dated is the label they write as old-fashioned: fail a sense used only in a formal, literary, dated (old-fashioned), or ironic register that states no register or another one (\"fine\" meaning not good at all, as in \"a fine friend you are\", is ironic), and fail a sense they label old-fashioned that is not labelled dated.\nOther senses of this entry, for reference:\n- (noun) \"An animal with feathers, wings, and a beak that lays eggs and is usually able to fly.\"\n- (noun) \"A girl or young woman.\"\n- (noun) \"A person of a particular type or character.\"\n- (noun) \"A shuttlecock used in badminton.\""}}}]},"context":{"subject":{"lemma":"bird","pos":"noun","gloss":"Time spent in prison.","siblings":[{"pos":"noun","gloss":"An animal with feathers, wings, and a beak that lays eggs and is usually able to fly."},{"pos":"noun","gloss":"A girl or young woman."},{"pos":"noun","gloss":"A person of a particular type or character."},{"pos":"noun","gloss":"A shuttlecock used in badminton."}]},"target":null,"topic":null,"attempts":[]},"reviewed_at":"2026-11-10T15:41:41.356Z"}', '2026-11-10T08:10:51.158Z', '2026-11-10T15:41:41.356Z', NULL);
